[SPEAKER_01]: But why, why would you need to, why would you need to establish it, when you said it's already there, and you have no choice but have it there?

403
00:24:24,714 --> 00:24:27,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but it's... What is it exactly that you're establishing then?

404
00:24:27,375 --> 00:24:44,225
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, I find that when I... that recognizing that, that image, that context, is like recognizing something that's already there, but that I don't always remember, I don't always

405
00:24:46,138 --> 00:24:53,482
[SPEAKER_01]: But as we described before, whether you get distracted by your past, future, whatever else, you can't actually be distracted from it.

406
00:24:53,923 --> 00:24:56,964
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how is then your forgetting of it...

407
00:24:57,125 --> 00:24:57,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignoring.

408
00:24:58,525 --> 00:25:03,028
[SPEAKER_01]: How is then your ignoring preventing you from understanding it?

409
00:25:04,376 --> 00:25:06,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that seems... Why is your ignoring a factor?

410
00:25:06,598 --> 00:25:08,199
[SPEAKER_00]: That seems to be what ignoring does.

411
00:25:08,239 --> 00:25:18,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring, then, the de facto response, if you're ignoring, is to assume ownership over something, which you've just...

412
00:25:18,468 --> 00:25:19,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Which you can't own.

413
00:25:19,468 --> 00:25:19,989
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't own.

414
00:25:20,129 --> 00:25:20,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

415
00:25:20,569 --> 00:25:22,571
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is that ownership, then, if you can't own it?

416
00:25:22,831 --> 00:25:23,412
[SPEAKER_00]: It's wrong.

417
00:25:23,592 --> 00:25:24,793
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely confused.

418
00:25:25,353 --> 00:25:28,156
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... it's...

419
00:25:28,216 --> 00:25:29,617
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why is it then still there?

420
00:25:35,993 --> 00:25:36,693
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a good question.

421
00:25:38,674 --> 00:25:39,134
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think?

422
00:25:41,434 --> 00:25:51,217
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it's impossible to step outside of the presently enduring moment of the presently enduring mental material image of the body...

423
00:25:51,237 --> 00:25:59,199
[SPEAKER_00]: There's obviously an element of wanting to own it, because there's a pleasure in it.

424
00:25:59,219 --> 00:25:59,699
[SPEAKER_00]: It don't matter.

425
00:26:01,079 --> 00:26:04,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting to own it makes no difference to the body that's unaware of you.

426
00:26:04,101 --> 00:26:04,841
[SPEAKER_01]: I know, yeah.

427
00:26:05,142 --> 00:26:13,246
[SPEAKER_01]: So, wanting to own it, not wanting to own it, accepting it, rejecting it, acting out of it, makes absolutely no difference to the matter, to the form, to... Because it's...

428
00:26:13,266 --> 00:26:14,727
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why is it still there?

429
00:26:14,967 --> 00:26:15,728
[SPEAKER_01]: That's my question.

430
00:26:15,908 --> 00:26:16,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

431
00:26:16,348 --> 00:26:17,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it still there?

432
00:26:33,634 --> 00:26:45,142
[SPEAKER_00]: All I can say is just by... because of not having accepted that, the nature of the body.

433
00:26:46,263 --> 00:26:49,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Not having seen it sufficiently enough.

434
00:26:49,525 --> 00:26:49,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

435
00:26:50,045 --> 00:26:51,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Not having seen it long enough.

436
00:26:52,007 --> 00:26:56,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Or as the Buddha said, not having seen it to the extent necessary for final knowledge.

437
00:26:57,090 --> 00:27:02,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, you haven't been seeing it long enough, literally,

438
00:27:03,095 --> 00:27:04,796
[SPEAKER_01]: That it's impossible to ignore it.

439
00:27:04,996 --> 00:27:05,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

440
00:27:05,796 --> 00:27:09,458
[SPEAKER_01]: And by virtue of that, that's why you're ignoring it.

441
00:27:09,518 --> 00:27:09,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

442
00:27:10,959 --> 00:27:18,283
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, the question is, so, why are you then not seeing it sufficiently enough?

443
00:27:19,803 --> 00:27:30,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Or in other words, since distraction can't take you away from the necessary base of your experience for distraction, or non-distraction, or whatever you choose to do,

444
00:27:32,379 --> 00:27:34,721
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you go about seeing it to the extent necessary?

445
00:27:38,004 --> 00:27:42,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the body to the extent necessary for final knowledge of removing all dispassion towards it?

446
00:27:42,347 --> 00:27:58,562
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess it's like dwelling, reminding, reminding myself about that, about the nature of it.

447
00:28:01,280 --> 00:28:07,264
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, the reminding of yourself, why would you be doing that?

448
00:28:08,745 --> 00:28:10,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it implies that you can't forget.

449
00:28:11,066 --> 00:28:12,447
[SPEAKER_00]: I know, yeah, exactly.

450
00:28:12,467 --> 00:28:12,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

451
00:28:26,333 --> 00:28:26,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you forget?

452
00:28:27,354 --> 00:28:28,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the thing.

453
00:28:28,114 --> 00:28:34,418
[SPEAKER_00]: There obviously is a forgetting involved in not seeing it for long enough.

454
00:28:35,158 --> 00:28:36,919
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the characteristics of forgetting?

455
00:28:39,300 --> 00:28:39,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Generally?

456
00:28:40,661 --> 00:28:41,802
[SPEAKER_01]: No, specifically.

457
00:28:49,308 --> 00:28:51,869
[SPEAKER_01]: What constitutes loss of mindfulness?

458
00:28:52,169 --> 00:29:01,373
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mindfulness is present in the enduring body, that's there, whether you think of it or not, whether past, future, whatever memories you have, it's there primarily.

459
00:29:01,753 --> 00:29:05,535
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot conceive experience outside of it or independent of it.

460
00:29:06,716 --> 00:29:06,976
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

461
00:29:09,137 --> 00:29:12,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Given that it's unforgettable, how can you forget about it?

462
00:29:15,561 --> 00:29:17,182
[SPEAKER_01]: By assuming that you can forget about it.

463
00:29:18,243 --> 00:29:21,304
[SPEAKER_00]: And how do you do that practically?

464
00:29:21,384 --> 00:29:23,005
[SPEAKER_00]: How does that make sense practically?

465
00:29:23,946 --> 00:29:24,686
[SPEAKER_00]: By just ignoring it.

466
00:29:24,706 --> 00:29:25,667
[SPEAKER_00]: By just not looking at it.

467
00:29:25,747 --> 00:29:30,870
[SPEAKER_00]: By... by... Well, again, not looking at it.

468
00:29:31,971 --> 00:29:32,511
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's there.

469
00:29:33,392 --> 00:29:33,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

470
00:29:34,352 --> 00:29:35,693
[SPEAKER_00]: So you cannot truly not look at it.

471
00:29:36,404 --> 00:29:44,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, no, you cannot look at it in the sense that you can choose to ignore the background.

472
00:29:47,078 --> 00:29:49,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, I choose to ignore this.

473
00:29:49,402 --> 00:29:49,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

474
00:29:50,082 --> 00:29:50,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Means it's still there.

475
00:29:51,004 --> 00:29:51,705
[SPEAKER_01]: It is still there.

476
00:29:51,845 --> 00:29:53,207
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't really ignore it.

477
00:29:53,847 --> 00:29:54,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Well,

478
00:29:56,159 --> 00:29:57,921
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you attempting at ignoring.

479
00:29:57,941 --> 00:29:59,644
[SPEAKER_01]: What is that involved?

480
00:29:59,704 --> 00:30:00,425
[SPEAKER_01]: What does that involve?

481
00:30:00,946 --> 00:30:02,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Doing something that you can't do.

482
00:30:02,909 --> 00:30:07,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that implies that you're doing something you can't actually do, yet you commit to it.

483
00:30:07,215 --> 00:30:07,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

484
00:30:07,956 --> 00:30:08,417
[SPEAKER_01]: To do it.

485
00:30:08,637 --> 00:30:08,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

486
00:30:09,931 --> 00:30:15,933
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the necessary basis for that type of commitment, of ignoring the unignorable?

487
00:30:15,993 --> 00:30:17,854
[SPEAKER_01]: No, more practically, more practically.

488
00:30:17,954 --> 00:30:26,638
[SPEAKER_01]: What is a common ground to whenever you choose to ignore or engage with something, although you know you can't own it, you can't have it, yet you do it?

489
00:30:26,758 --> 00:30:28,918
[SPEAKER_00]: A lack of authenticity, lack of faith.

490
00:30:29,619 --> 00:30:31,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, again, that's on the level of the view.

491
00:30:32,720 --> 00:30:36,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically, like, say, you want to distract yourself now.

492
00:30:37,102 --> 00:30:37,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you do that?

493
00:30:40,578 --> 00:30:44,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember, whenever you decided to distract yourself, why did you distract yourself?

494
00:30:46,760 --> 00:30:47,981
[SPEAKER_01]: What was the reason for that?

495
00:30:48,981 --> 00:30:52,902
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that, oh, because I'm inauthentic... No, none of those can be your motivations.

496
00:30:53,323 --> 00:30:54,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's... What is pleasant?

497
00:30:55,964 --> 00:30:56,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing it.

498
00:30:56,524 --> 00:30:56,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing it.

499
00:30:58,064 --> 00:31:00,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you see it as pleasant.

500
00:31:00,766 --> 00:31:05,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it's always, basically, a matter of choice upon how you feel.

501
00:31:07,465 --> 00:31:09,626
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why forgetfulness is not a problem.

502
00:31:10,707 --> 00:31:17,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Genuinely forgetting things, it's within present enduring mindfulness of the of the mental material body.

503
00:31:18,172 --> 00:31:27,978
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the choice you make within that, which means you're fully aware of it, that is rooted in how you feel, which is basically a choice rooted in desire.

504
00:31:28,859 --> 00:31:30,360
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the common ground.

505
00:31:30,920 --> 00:31:33,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire for more, desire for less, desire for neither.

506
00:31:35,721 --> 00:31:41,049
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha didn't say like, oh, you don't forget every moment of your life, watch every second of it.

507
00:31:41,089 --> 00:31:41,170
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

508
00:31:41,190 --> 00:31:43,914
[SPEAKER_01]: He just said, avoid unwholesome and cultivate wholesome.

509
00:31:45,276 --> 00:31:46,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't choose anything.

510
00:31:48,657 --> 00:31:51,478
[SPEAKER_01]: rooted in greed, aversion and distraction.

511
00:31:51,598 --> 00:31:52,838
[SPEAKER_01]: And it will result in what?

512
00:31:52,878 --> 00:32:00,380
[SPEAKER_01]: It will result in you ceasing to maintain gratuitous notion of ignoring that which is unignorable.

513
00:32:01,821 --> 00:32:05,001
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, once you fully develop that, you cannot ignore it again.

514
00:32:05,041 --> 00:32:05,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

515
00:32:05,602 --> 00:32:10,583
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the process of uprooting ignorance is utterly, completely irreversible.

516
00:32:11,448 --> 00:32:12,748
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot then start ignoring.

517
00:32:12,788 --> 00:32:12,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

518
00:32:13,008 --> 00:32:22,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Because what you thought ignoring was, as in, when you just forget, or you decided something, you're doing something else, you can't think of everything to the same extent at all times.

519
00:32:22,992 --> 00:32:25,232
[SPEAKER_01]: But you thought that that was the reason for your ignorance.

520
00:32:25,292 --> 00:32:25,712
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't.

521
00:32:25,812 --> 00:32:26,733
[SPEAKER_01]: The desire is.

522
00:32:27,193 --> 00:32:28,233
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is always conscious.

523
00:32:28,733 --> 00:32:40,857
[SPEAKER_01]: You can never accidentally stumble upon decisions rooted in desire, actions that you perform on account of desire, whether for more, for less, aversion, delusion, distraction, whatever.

524
00:32:42,735 --> 00:32:59,836
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why... the reason why I'm coming back to this is because that's pretty much like a... one of the main stumbling blocks for whoever starts, or even like later to develop it, is assuming that you must be in this... like maintain this clarity of this is unownable, this is unownable, this is unownable every second of your life.

525
00:33:00,420 --> 00:33:04,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Which kind of implies that you can still lose it.

526
00:33:04,581 --> 00:33:06,542
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't lose it when you don't think about it.

527
00:33:07,122 --> 00:33:09,343
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't lose it when you go to sleep.

528
00:33:09,383 --> 00:33:14,725
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't lose it when you choose to do something of... that's not rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

529
00:33:16,145 --> 00:33:16,946
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's another thing.

530
00:33:16,966 --> 00:33:20,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when people don't know where the problem is, then they blame everything to be the problem.

531
00:33:21,067 --> 00:33:22,227
[SPEAKER_01]: I mustn't do anything.

532
00:33:22,308 --> 00:33:23,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't that craving?

533
00:33:23,348 --> 00:33:25,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't that a desire?

534
00:33:25,789 --> 00:33:27,469
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to go for... well, that's craving.

535
00:33:28,049 --> 00:33:28,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, that's attachment.

536
00:33:29,030 --> 00:33:29,410
[SPEAKER_01]: It isn't.

537
00:33:31,878 --> 00:33:45,146
[SPEAKER_01]: What craving is, what desire is, what attachment is, whatever you want to call it, is when you decide to act upon desire, upon aversion, or upon need for distraction.

538
00:33:45,487 --> 00:33:50,810
[SPEAKER_01]: When you choose to act on account of how you feel, in regard to how you feel.

539
00:33:52,911 --> 00:33:55,573
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you feel pleasantly, you want to protect it,

540
00:33:56,627 --> 00:33:57,808
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't have more of it.

541
00:33:58,349 --> 00:33:59,510
[SPEAKER_00]: You choose to act.

542
00:33:59,610 --> 00:34:04,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, having thoughts of protecting it is not on the same level as choosing to act out of it.

543
00:34:04,615 --> 00:34:12,842
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you maintain this completely gratuitously, wrongly assumed order that we just described, that it's actually unassumable.

544
00:34:13,563 --> 00:34:18,427
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you maintain your gratuitous ownership as well, of things that you know are unownable.

545
00:34:19,388 --> 00:34:20,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, your ownership is there.

546
00:34:21,909 --> 00:34:28,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you still don't know what the basis for that ownership is, and you're scalelessly maintaining the base of the ownership.

547
00:34:28,514 --> 00:34:35,280
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if you look at the base of the ownership, you'll say, ah, it's... it's not having enough mindfulness, it's distraction, it's this, it's this, it's this.

548
00:34:35,660 --> 00:34:40,464
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that is fully understood, to the point of, like as I said, distraction is not forgetting.

549
00:34:41,234 --> 00:34:48,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Distraction is basically choosing to distract yourself through the means of desire, through the means of aversion, through the means of distraction.

550
00:34:48,480 --> 00:34:49,641
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what distraction is.

551
00:34:50,062 --> 00:34:53,785
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're about to do something, oh, I must stop doing, no?

552
00:34:54,425 --> 00:34:55,887
[SPEAKER_01]: I must know where it is rooted.

553
00:34:56,687 --> 00:34:58,889
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how I will stop maintaining the basis.

554
00:34:59,169 --> 00:35:00,711
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't stop doing things.

555
00:35:02,912 --> 00:35:04,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's another doing, which is not doing.

556
00:35:05,193 --> 00:35:13,656
[SPEAKER_00]: So, am I doing this in order to... because of a particular feeling in regard to how I'm feeling right now?

557
00:35:13,696 --> 00:35:22,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, if you're acting out of how you're feeling, based on that lack of context, you will be acting out of craving.

558
00:35:24,560 --> 00:35:25,140
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are, sorry?

559
00:35:25,280 --> 00:35:28,141
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're acting on the basis of how you're feeling, you will be acting out of craving.

560
00:35:28,321 --> 00:35:32,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah

561
00:35:33,543 --> 00:35:47,813
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're acting... as I said, like, see, people are not even aware of most... many of the actions, because you're only aware of the ones that are based on your desire or things you want, you know, but you do millions of things in the meantime.

562
00:35:48,514 --> 00:35:49,855
[SPEAKER_01]: You open the door, you close the door.

563
00:35:49,975 --> 00:35:52,197
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those are little choices which you make.

564
00:35:52,237 --> 00:35:56,340
[SPEAKER_01]: They are kind of neither wholesome or unwholesome, which means they are not unwholesome.

565
00:35:57,320 --> 00:35:58,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you can do it.

566
00:35:58,401 --> 00:36:03,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to get paralyzed about what do I do, what do I do then, what do I do, because all the doing is the problem.

567
00:36:03,086 --> 00:36:03,267
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

568
00:36:03,767 --> 00:36:12,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Specific type of doing, doing that's rooted in desire, doing that's rooted in craving in regard to how you're presently feeling while you're doing it, those are the ones you shouldn't do.

569
00:36:14,918 --> 00:36:15,538
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I said.

570
00:36:15,558 --> 00:36:22,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Let those... when the monks tried... some monks tried to make him make those austere practices mandatory for everyone.

571
00:36:22,721 --> 00:36:24,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody should say no to this.

572
00:36:24,321 --> 00:36:24,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody should say...

573
00:36:25,241 --> 00:36:25,682
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, no.

574
00:36:26,342 --> 00:36:31,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Let those who want to say no to such and such life, say no to that, to like living under... living in the...

575
00:36:32,344 --> 00:36:33,684
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know, roof dwellings.

576
00:36:33,704 --> 00:36:37,285
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let those who want to eat one meal a day, eat one meal a day.

577
00:36:37,305 --> 00:36:40,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Let those who want to wear hand robes, wear hand robes.

578
00:36:40,486 --> 00:36:42,367
[SPEAKER_01]: And those who don't want to, not to do it.

579
00:36:43,167 --> 00:36:48,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whatever... what specifically you're doing, only goes so far.

580
00:36:48,971 --> 00:36:59,699
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you specifically must abstain from... from sensual indulgence, chasing sense... sense pleasures, sexual intercourse and all these other things, if you want full enlightenment.

581
00:37:00,580 --> 00:37:05,323
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean, like, you remain like a motionless log for the rest of your life, until you become an Arahant.

582
00:37:06,024 --> 00:37:08,746
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it means that you just don't do things that are rooted in desire.

583
00:37:11,155 --> 00:37:17,480
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's another... the simile of the... the bait, the herd, the herd sutta in the Majjhima.

584
00:37:17,600 --> 00:37:18,260
[SPEAKER_01]: The bait, yeah.

585
00:37:18,421 --> 00:37:20,002
[SPEAKER_01]: The bait about the deer herd.

586
00:37:21,123 --> 00:37:21,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

587
00:37:21,283 --> 00:37:25,246
[SPEAKER_01]: The first one just takes everything, the second one rejects everything.

588
00:37:26,987 --> 00:37:38,416
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the wise one is the one that realizes, you know, so you have to eat, as in you have to act, you have to remain engaged to some extent, in terms of breathing, walking, sitting, lying down.

589
00:37:38,556 --> 00:37:39,817
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those are choices you make.

590
00:37:40,859 --> 00:37:48,965
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only the choices that are rooted in desire, which is basically craving in regard to how you feel, that's what you don't do.

591
00:37:49,866 --> 00:37:51,727
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why Mahārāja will be unable to see you.

592
00:37:52,148 --> 00:37:53,429
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you will become free.

593
00:37:53,869 --> 00:37:54,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you stopped eating.

594
00:37:55,030 --> 00:37:55,810
[SPEAKER_01]: You removed all of that.

595
00:37:55,830 --> 00:37:56,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that's attachment.

596
00:37:57,512 --> 00:37:59,173
[SPEAKER_00]: And no one will see the difference.

597
00:37:59,313 --> 00:38:00,894
[SPEAKER_01]: And externally, nobody can measure you.

598
00:38:01,334 --> 00:38:01,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

599
00:38:02,635 --> 00:38:05,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Including devas and brāhmaṇas can't measure you, because you don't manifest.

600
00:38:09,424 --> 00:38:11,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone Putrajana will be able to see you and pay you respects.

601
00:38:14,205 --> 00:38:16,065
[SPEAKER_01]: There are no criteria for what enlightenment is.

602
00:38:16,205 --> 00:38:17,186
[SPEAKER_01]: How can he possibly know?

603
00:38:19,286 --> 00:38:32,949
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's so important, because unless you clarify this, the characteristics of the present enduring body right here, right now, even when you forget, when you remember, you realize this is there.

604
00:38:32,969 --> 00:38:38,190
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a necessary basis for any experience, including experience of forgetting, including the experience of desire.

605
00:38:39,607 --> 00:38:54,076
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about uprooting and finding this universal kind of hidden truth that holds the whole set up on its palm, and now you got it all, basically, because you transcended everything.

606
00:38:54,176 --> 00:38:54,336
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

607
00:38:55,577 --> 00:38:58,779
[SPEAKER_01]: It's what you were given, stop misconceiving it.

608
00:39:00,327 --> 00:39:04,730
[SPEAKER_01]: And you stop misconceiving it when you fully understood that it's impossible to misconceive it.

609
00:39:04,830 --> 00:39:07,311
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, whether you conceive it or not, it makes no difference.

610
00:39:07,411 --> 00:39:10,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Things are like this, to this extent.

611
00:39:11,794 --> 00:39:15,876
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the law of impermanence and these things, the universal principles.

612
00:39:17,997 --> 00:39:20,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you say, okay, so how do I stop misconceiving it?

613
00:39:20,959 --> 00:39:22,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, by not being forgetful and so on.

614
00:39:22,760 --> 00:39:24,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but why is forgetfulness the problem?

615
00:39:24,902 --> 00:39:25,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Why did I do that?

616
00:39:25,902 --> 00:39:27,063
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because of the desire.

617
00:39:27,799 --> 00:39:28,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I guess people...

618
00:39:28,740 --> 00:39:39,145
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess the tendency is to kind of... when you suddenly become mindful, let's say, you might go, oh, yes, I've not been mindful for a while.

619
00:39:40,106 --> 00:39:53,073
[SPEAKER_00]: I must be mindful from now on, rather than... rather than recognizing that, hang on a minute, I'm mindful of something now that cannot have been there even while I wasn't being mindful.

620
00:39:53,513 --> 00:39:53,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

621
00:39:54,233 --> 00:39:57,615
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of you, when you become mindful,

622
00:39:58,920 --> 00:40:03,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Developing the view that that's what mindfulness is, and you have to develop more of it.

623
00:40:03,463 --> 00:40:09,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead, don't take their view and start questioning further that which you discovered through your first attempt at mindfulness.

624
00:40:11,100 --> 00:40:17,984
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when a person has a first attempt at mindfulness, they have a set of how it's felt, how it's perceived, what are its characteristics, and they take it for granted.

625
00:40:19,124 --> 00:40:31,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, the whole point is to not take and keep going inwardly, as in digging inwardly further and further, not like, oh, no, now I know what mindfulness is, now I just need to develop this mindfulness that I know what it is, to the extent necessary to be an arahant.

626
00:40:32,391 --> 00:40:38,414
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the extent necessary means the extent necessary of understanding it to the necessary extent.

627
00:40:38,434 --> 00:40:39,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the mindfulness is about...

628
00:40:40,681 --> 00:40:45,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it's like... Yeah, for most people, they think, like, even Puthujana knows what mindfulness is.

629
00:40:45,425 --> 00:40:46,505
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just when you're present.

630
00:40:46,705 --> 00:40:51,549
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he's recognizing a phenomenon, but you have to understand what that phenomenon is, you know.

631
00:40:51,609 --> 00:40:52,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

632
00:40:52,029 --> 00:40:54,711
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're properly mindful once you understand what mindfulness is.

633
00:40:54,731 --> 00:40:54,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

634
00:40:56,052 --> 00:41:02,237
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you develop that understanding to the extent necessary, that's how all the unwholesome things will fall off.

635
00:41:02,317 --> 00:41:05,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Or can you say, you're only mindful of the body when you understand what the body is?

636
00:41:08,301 --> 00:41:09,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness is the body.

637
00:41:09,801 --> 00:41:10,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

638
00:41:11,182 --> 00:41:12,283
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on you doing it.

639
00:41:13,044 --> 00:41:16,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding of the necessary base for experience is what mindfulness is.

640
00:41:16,246 --> 00:41:23,492
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the four foundations of mindfulness, basically understanding the four foundations of your experience, is the four foundations of mindfulness.

641
00:41:24,192 --> 00:41:30,618
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point I'm making, is people think they can do mindfulness, do meditation technique, do their observance.

642
00:41:32,042 --> 00:41:36,124
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only reason they can do it is because their view conceives them as doable.

643
00:41:36,885 --> 00:41:40,307
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why they'll never approve their wrong view for as long as they do it like that.

644
00:41:42,268 --> 00:41:52,014
[SPEAKER_01]: So, fully understanding the necessary basis of your experience, the present in yours, whether you ignore it or not, makes your mindfulness impeccable.

645
00:41:52,054 --> 00:41:52,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever comes...

646
00:41:52,634 --> 00:41:53,275
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, literally.

647
00:41:54,279 --> 00:41:56,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness, the Buddha said, mindfulness is properly developed.

648
00:41:57,000 --> 00:42:02,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, things of extreme nature will not be able to perturb it, because, again, it doesn't matter how extreme.

649
00:42:03,401 --> 00:42:05,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Big, small, far, near, heavy, light.

650
00:42:05,842 --> 00:42:10,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever your experience is, this is presently, during, there is a necessary basis for it.

651
00:42:10,723 --> 00:42:11,263
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

652
00:42:11,523 --> 00:42:17,384
[SPEAKER_01]: The lamp of body, which is your universe, which is your whole container, which is like... has nothing...

653
00:42:17,884 --> 00:42:20,545
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no relationship of... that you can

654
00:42:21,203 --> 00:42:24,085
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't conceive on the same footing as it is, as that body.

655
00:42:24,545 --> 00:42:28,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything you can conceive, it's the words, against, before, after, it.

656
00:42:28,908 --> 00:42:30,589
[SPEAKER_01]: But nothing on the level of it.

657
00:42:32,009 --> 00:42:35,612
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why even you can't put it in space and say it's in my head or it's in...

658
00:42:35,672 --> 00:42:38,974
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do assume that, means you're not seeing its nature.

659
00:42:42,656 --> 00:42:46,338
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any movement in regard to it will be secondary and that was never a problem.

660
00:42:46,438 --> 00:42:49,440
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that your mind must stop moving, because that will become your movement.

661
00:42:50,755 --> 00:42:57,442
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, stop misconceiving that which is inconceivable, or stop conceiving the inconceivable.

662
00:42:58,322 --> 00:42:59,263
[SPEAKER_01]: What is inconceivable?

663
00:42:59,984 --> 00:43:06,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Form is inconceivable, feeling is inconceivable, perception is inconceivable, your intentions are inconceivable, consciousness is inconceivable.

664
00:43:09,534 --> 00:43:14,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable means you have removed your misconceivings.

665
00:43:15,699 --> 00:43:20,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when people are inconceivable, they fail to see that that becomes their new conceiving.

666
00:43:21,024 --> 00:43:22,945
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't think the inconceivable.

667
00:43:23,766 --> 00:43:25,768
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only stop conceiving it.

668
00:43:28,470 --> 00:43:35,376
[SPEAKER_01]: And you stop conceiving it not by developing pure awareness of the presently enduring magical moment of your mindfulness.

669
00:43:36,217 --> 00:43:43,403
[SPEAKER_01]: You basically stop conceiving it through acting out of desire,

670
00:43:44,835 --> 00:43:50,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out of craving, acting out of intentional choice or ignorance.

671
00:43:55,463 --> 00:43:56,464
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's doable.

672
00:43:57,845 --> 00:43:59,926
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, oh, when will enlightenment happen to me?

673
00:43:59,946 --> 00:44:01,087
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been doing it for twenty...

674
00:44:01,107 --> 00:44:05,790
[SPEAKER_01]: If you've been doing it for twenty years, and you don't even have the right view, you're obviously doing it wrong.

675
00:44:06,731 --> 00:44:11,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Because as the Buddha said, it's impossible, if somebody's doing these things rightly, for the results to not take place.

676
00:44:13,493 --> 00:44:20,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if a hen sits on her eggs, even if she doesn't wish, may my chicks pierce out of the shell safely, they will, because she sits on them.

677
00:44:22,940 --> 00:44:30,246
[SPEAKER_01]: But if a hen thinks she's sitting on her eggs, but no chicks are piercing out, maybe she should reevaluate what constitutes sitting.

678
00:44:32,628 --> 00:44:33,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe I'm sitting in the wrong place.

679
00:44:34,809 --> 00:44:35,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

680
00:44:35,610 --> 00:44:39,193
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, it is like, again, like, what is the purpose of your practice?

681
00:44:39,996 --> 00:44:47,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Of any form of commitment to the dhamma as a lay person or as a monk, okay, you want to achieve something.

682
00:44:47,422 --> 00:44:47,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

683
00:44:48,103 --> 00:44:49,224
[SPEAKER_01]: How are you going about it?

684
00:44:51,786 --> 00:45:05,037
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's, oh, I want to achieve, so I have no lack of clarity of what I want, further lack of clarity of how I'm going about it, and yet, here I am, fully committed to my meditation retreat and practice and just have expect things to occur to me.

685
00:45:06,580 --> 00:45:11,381
[SPEAKER_01]: And measure, basically, success by the experience of pleasure I get from it.

686
00:45:11,521 --> 00:45:15,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

687
00:45:17,302 --> 00:45:20,622
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is the it-body, did we start describing this?

688
00:45:20,922 --> 00:45:21,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

689
00:45:22,883 --> 00:45:23,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Still there.

690
00:45:24,423 --> 00:45:27,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Furthermore, have its characteristics changed?

691
00:45:31,364 --> 00:45:31,624
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

692
00:45:32,164 --> 00:45:32,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

693
00:45:33,985 --> 00:45:36,065
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another reason why people mistake it into

694
00:45:37,581 --> 00:45:38,561
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it always remains the same.

695
00:45:38,801 --> 00:45:39,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

696
00:45:39,241 --> 00:45:40,802
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start assuming it's unchangeable.

697
00:45:41,942 --> 00:45:42,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I guess.

698
00:45:43,062 --> 00:45:43,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Or invisible.

699
00:45:43,822 --> 00:45:45,242
[SPEAKER_00]: You just... Oh, you stop seeing it.

700
00:45:45,342 --> 00:45:45,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

701
00:45:45,762 --> 00:45:46,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

702
00:45:46,162 --> 00:45:46,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

703
00:45:46,503 --> 00:45:47,223
[SPEAKER_01]: That's actually, yeah.

704
00:45:47,463 --> 00:45:49,223
[SPEAKER_01]: That's even more so, isn't it?

705
00:45:49,943 --> 00:45:52,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

706
00:45:52,424 --> 00:45:53,564
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're still subjected to it.

707
00:45:54,324 --> 00:46:00,145
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still that... a certain... opaque lamp that resists you.

708
00:46:00,365 --> 00:46:00,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Mm-hmm.

709
00:46:02,645 --> 00:46:03,385
[SPEAKER_00]: That my whole life.

710
00:46:03,505 --> 00:46:04,586
[SPEAKER_00]: My everything depends on.

711
00:46:06,266 --> 00:46:07,046
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's still an image.

712
00:46:07,875 --> 00:46:08,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

713
00:46:09,737 --> 00:46:11,058
[SPEAKER_01]: And you cannot even think anything...

714
00:46:11,939 --> 00:46:12,259
[SPEAKER_01]: There you go.

715
00:46:12,279 --> 00:46:14,221
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't even finish that sentence without finding an image.

716
00:46:14,441 --> 00:46:14,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

717
00:46:17,024 --> 00:46:18,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you know that body if there is no image of it?

718
00:46:19,406 --> 00:46:19,586
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

719
00:46:19,726 --> 00:46:19,886
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

720
00:46:20,247 --> 00:46:20,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

721
00:46:22,108 --> 00:46:23,550
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only means.

722
00:46:23,690 --> 00:46:28,074
[SPEAKER_01]: The only means are means that are already materially mental or mentally material.

723
00:46:28,815 --> 00:46:30,716
[SPEAKER_01]: That's nāmarūpa, basically, is the definition.

724
00:46:30,777 --> 00:46:30,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

725
00:46:32,644 --> 00:46:39,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if there is no nāma of it, if there is no designated aspect of the present enduring lamp, it's inconceivable, as in, it's not manifested.

726
00:46:40,130 --> 00:46:42,972
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't, whether you conceive it or stop conceiving it, you can't do anything.

727
00:46:44,773 --> 00:46:50,878
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is nothing to be designated, that's when people say, ah, it's all in your head, it's all in your mind.

728
00:46:50,958 --> 00:46:52,519
[SPEAKER_00]: It isn't.

729
00:46:52,719 --> 00:46:59,684
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all in your mind, or it's all in the world, are already on the level of your mind, which is mentally material.

730
00:47:00,225 --> 00:47:00,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

731
00:47:02,698 --> 00:47:04,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is something you are subjected to.

732
00:47:07,821 --> 00:47:10,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, therefore, there is that resistance aspect to it.

733
00:47:11,183 --> 00:47:11,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

734
00:47:12,144 --> 00:47:14,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you had no say in how that image manifests.

735
00:47:15,046 --> 00:47:17,147
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only discern its given characteristics.

736
00:47:18,548 --> 00:47:26,633
[SPEAKER_01]: If you truly were responsible for concocting it with your designation, with your naming, as people sometimes try to blame the naming for the issue,

737
00:47:28,050 --> 00:47:33,075
[SPEAKER_01]: You would then be responsible for the creation of its characteristics, which wouldn't be its characteristics.

738
00:47:33,415 --> 00:47:37,340
[SPEAKER_00]: There would be no it to designate.

739
00:47:37,560 --> 00:47:46,870
[SPEAKER_00]: Even like an artist, if they draw some paint or something, if you ask them where did it come from, they say, oh, I just drew

740
00:47:48,078 --> 00:47:50,419
[SPEAKER_00]: That thing that... Sure.

741
00:47:51,119 --> 00:47:52,160
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm secondary, you know.

742
00:47:52,240 --> 00:47:53,140
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't really create it.

743
00:47:53,160 --> 00:47:58,322
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's the thing, like, as I said, all of the five aggregates are indifferent to you.

744
00:47:59,462 --> 00:48:00,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Your feelings, your own intentions.

745
00:48:01,323 --> 00:48:14,268
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to... the way we examine the nature present during body, take up... it's slightly subtler to examine, so I usually just stick to the body, because people might be hearing this, so I just get... it's already hard enough.

746
00:48:14,928 --> 00:48:21,697
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do develop skills, you can do the same examination, the same type of reflection and contemplation in regard to your presently doing intention.

747
00:48:21,717 --> 00:48:28,786
[SPEAKER_01]: When you have intention to do something, what is the... what are the characteristics of the image that's necessarily contained for your intention?

748
00:48:29,267 --> 00:48:30,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is the intention?

749
00:48:31,950 --> 00:48:32,872
[SPEAKER_01]: You're subjected to it.

750
00:48:33,847 --> 00:48:35,148
[SPEAKER_01]: You chose to attend it.

751
00:48:35,688 --> 00:48:38,190
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only choose to attend that which is given.

752
00:48:38,931 --> 00:48:47,337
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the whole thing can be boiled down, as the Buddha said, to correctly attending things or incorrectly attending things.

753
00:48:48,278 --> 00:48:52,321
[SPEAKER_01]: Not correctly manifesting and creating and fabricating or incorrectly.

754
00:48:52,361 --> 00:48:52,541
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

755
00:48:53,442 --> 00:48:57,805
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that which is given on its own, that which arises, manifests, which endures on its own.

756
00:48:58,626 --> 00:49:01,168
[SPEAKER_01]: You have a choice to attend properly or improperly.

757
00:49:02,290 --> 00:49:13,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Improper attention results in increasing of sensuality and decreasing of non-sensuality, increasing of hate and decreasing of non-hate, increasing of ignorance and decreasing of non-ignorance.

758
00:49:13,753 --> 00:49:15,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Proper attention results in opposite.

759
00:49:16,253 --> 00:49:26,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Decreasing of sensuality and increasing of non-sensuality, decreasing of hate and increasing of non-hate, and decreasing of ignorance and increasing of non-ignorance.

760
00:49:28,876 --> 00:49:30,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you want it or not, it's not up to you.

761
00:49:31,472 --> 00:49:33,433
[SPEAKER_01]: What is up to you, is what you choose to attend to.

762
00:49:33,633 --> 00:49:33,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

763
00:49:36,074 --> 00:49:38,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Things rooted in desire, or things rooted in non-desire?

764
00:49:39,136 --> 00:49:44,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Things rooted in trying to change how you feel, or things rooted in not trying to change how you feel?

765
00:49:56,304 --> 00:49:57,365
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only reason that

766
00:49:58,853 --> 00:50:03,937
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is still not free from gratuitous conceivings that are actually impossible to conceive.

767
00:50:05,978 --> 00:50:08,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Is... because of the choices you make.

768
00:50:10,902 --> 00:50:20,530
[SPEAKER_01]: And is... is there anybody, anywhere in the universe, in the past, or in the future, other than you, that can be responsible for those choices?

769
00:50:20,550 --> 00:50:20,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

770
00:50:22,071 --> 00:50:22,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Inconceivable.

771
00:50:22,771 --> 00:50:22,911
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

772
00:50:23,072 --> 00:50:24,613
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another inconceivable thing.

773
00:50:24,653 --> 00:50:24,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

774
00:50:26,708 --> 00:50:32,809
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable that anybody else can be owner of your kāma, which is a choice.

775
00:50:34,710 --> 00:50:37,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Not a mystical energy, just in case people are not there.

776
00:50:39,831 --> 00:50:41,771
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's inconceivable that... and that's what it's meant by.

777
00:50:42,931 --> 00:50:46,312
[SPEAKER_01]: You are the sole owner of your choices and the results of those choices.

778
00:50:46,732 --> 00:50:48,532
[SPEAKER_01]: The result being mind still not being free.

779
